"Apply the three questions of the first lesson on this shelf. On the conclusion, Hume is closest about causes and Whitehead about things. Hume and the Buddha agree that causation is regular dependence and that no further power is found behind it. Whitehead and the Buddha agree that what looks like an enduring thing is a pattern of dependent events. Mackie supplies what both leave unsaid, that the dependence is always on many conditions together.": "5c52365705a4",
"Between a feeling and the craving that follows it there is a small gap. Practice is learning to notice it.": "dec01c8a76ba",
"Dependent origination is not fate. A thing that depends on conditions can be changed by changing them.": "87b45959541d",
"Empty means empty of independent existence. A flower is full of sun, rain, soil, and time.": "f7a83dfadb88",
"Hume asked what we observe when one billiard ball strikes another. We see the first move, the contact, and the second move. We do not see a power passing between them. All events seem entirely loose and separate. One event follows another; but we never can observe any tie between them. They seem conjoined, but never connected. A cause, on his account, is an object regularly followed by another, and he added a second definition in other words: where, if the first object had not been, the second never had existed. Set that beside when this does not exist, that does not come to be. Both describe causation as dependence that can be relied on, with no inner force required. They differ in confidence. Hume concluded that our belief in causes rests on custom and cannot be justified by reason. The Buddha treated the regularity as a discovered law and built a practice on it.": "91aa9d4d6454",
"Hume noticed that we never see a force passing from cause to effect. The Buddha agreed, and then asked which conditions we can change.": "602975f99cf0",
"Nothing happens from one cause. A fire needs a spark, and it also needs fuel and air.": "8a110f588a64",
"On the argument, the three reach these views by different roads. Hume looked for the source of an idea and failed to find it. Whitehead built a system to fit modern physics. The Buddha examined how grief and craving arise in his own experience. On the purpose, the likeness ends. Hume said that his doubts, strong in the study, vanished when he dined and played backgammon with his friends, and he thought this a good thing. For the Buddha the analysis was made in order to be used. If suffering has conditions, and the conditions can be found, the effect can be removed by removing them. Western philosophy studied causation chiefly to understand how we know nature. The Buddha studied it to find out where a chain can be cut.": "58a72cdc9b28",
"The Buddhas formula is very simple: when this is present, that comes to be; when this is absent, that does not.": "e2825643638b",
"The first further step is practical. The twelve links are not equally within reach. No one can decide to stop feeling pleasure and pain when the senses meet their objects. Between feeling and craving there is a gap. A pleasant feeling arises, and wanting more of it usually follows so quickly that the two seem one. With training in attention the feeling can be known as a feeling before the wanting takes hold, and at that point the next link does not form. The teachers of the tradition point to this joint as the place where practice does its work. A theory of causation, in Buddhist hands, becomes an instruction about where to look.": "719addeceedd",
"The particular application is a chain of twelve links that traces how suffering is produced. Ignorance is a requirement for choices. Choices are a requirement for consciousness. Consciousness is a requirement for name and form, and so on through the six senses, contact, feeling, craving, grasping, continued existence, and rebirth to old age and death. Each link is a condition for the next. It is not said to be its sole cause. The monk Sāriputta explained the relation with a simile: Suppose there were two sheaves of reeds leaning up against each other. Each stands because of the other, and if the first of those sheaves of reeds were to be pulled away, the other would collapse. The chain is given in order to be run backward. When ignorance fades away and ceases with no residue left behind, choices cease, and so on to the ceasing of this entire mass of suffering.": "457f715ee4be",
"The second step is philosophical, and it was taken by Nāgārjuna about six centuries after the Buddha. If a thing arises only in dependence on conditions, it has no nature that is its own apart from them. That absence of own-nature is what he meant by emptiness. His best-known verse says that whatever arises from conditions is what he calls empty, that this is a provisional designation, and that this is the middle way. He drew the consequence: no thing whatever arises without conditions, and so there is nothing that is not empty. Emptiness in this sense is dependent origination seen from the other side. It is the opposite of nothingness, since only what lacks a fixed nature can arise, change, and cease. Chinese Buddhists later pictured the result as a net of jewels in which each jewel reflects all the others. Hume stopped at the loose and separate events. Nāgārjuna went on to say that the events too have no being apart from their relations, which is a step Whitehead would recognize.": "e9c2b020d3ea",
"The teaching has a general form and a particular application. The general form is one sentence: When this exists, this comes to be; due to the arising of this, this arises. Its reverse follows: when this does not exist, that does not come to be, and with the ceasing of this, that ceases. Nothing arises by itself, from nothing, or from a single cause. Whatever arises does so in dependence on conditions, and lasts as long as they do. The Buddha presented this as a fact about the world that he had found and had not made. Whether Realized Ones arise or not, this law of nature persists, this regularity of natural principles, this surety of natural principles, specific conditionality.": "61ed54bd7f0f",
"The teaching is also called the middle. A monk named Kaccāna asked what right view is, and the Buddha answered: All exists: this is one extreme. All does not exist: this is the second extreme. Avoiding these two extremes, the Realized One teaches by the middle way, and he recited the twelve links. What arises dependently is not nothing, since it arises and has effects. It does not exist in its own right either, since it is there only while its conditions are. When Ānanda remarked that dependent origination, though deep, seemed to him as plain as can be, the Buddha replied: Dont say that, Ānanda, dont say that! Elsewhere the tradition reports him as saying that one who sees dependent origination sees the teaching.": "83de47ffe56e",
"Western listeners often meet this teaching as everything is connected, a phrase that is pleasant, vague, and impossible to act on. Others hear the word cause and think of fate. Both miss what the Buddha was doing. Begin with something small and checkable, such as a moment of irritation, and ask what had to be in place for it: a tired body, a noise, an expectation, a habit of reacting. Then ask which of these could have been otherwise. The listener has now seen the two halves of the teaching, that things arise from many conditions and that some of the conditions are within reach. The twelve links and emptiness can come afterward.": "4a5fecdd30e4",
"When investigators say that a short circuit caused a fire, they do not mean that it was enough by itself. There had to be oxygen, something flammable nearby, and no sprinkler. They do not mean that it was necessary either, since other things can start fires. Mackie proposed that what we call a cause is an insufficient but necessary part of a set of conditions that is together sufficient, though other sets would also have done. We pick one member of the set and call it the cause because it interests us. This is the analysis the reed simile requires. Each link of the twelve is a condition among others, and the tradition says in so many words that many conditions give rise to each thing. Mackie was analyzing ordinary talk about causes. He drew no conclusion about suffering.": "a1aeb75de9dd",
"Whitehead, who had written Principia Mathematica with Russell, turned in later life to metaphysics and rejected the picture of a world made of enduring substances that have properties. The basic units of reality, he proposed, are momentary events, which he called actual occasions. Each one arises by taking account of the occasions before it, comes to completion, and becomes in its turn a condition for those that follow. What we call a thing, a stone or a person, is a society of such events with a common pattern. Relation comes first on this view, and the thing is what the relations compose. Of Western systems this is the nearest to the Buddhist analysis of persons and things into dependently arising events, and Whitehead himself remarked on the kinship. He kept a God in his system, as the source of order and novelty, where the Buddhist account has none.": "e9d68bf53c63"
}
